>> title: Tortilla Española
>> category: Eggs
>> tags: eggs, potatoes, spanish, one-pot, skillet, tapas
>> counters: One Pot
>> aka: tortilla de patatas, Spanish omelette, Spanish tortilla, potato omelette, tortilla de papas
>> servings: 4
>> time: 1 hr
>> step.1: One pan, one bowl and a plate to turn it on. The oil is poured off rather than strained, and the potato is lifted straight into the beaten egg with a slotted spoon — nothing here needs a colander, which is the only reason a fried-potato dish can sit on this shelf at all.
>> step.2: fry gently 20 min, no colour
>> step.3: beat, lift the potato in, stand 10 min
>> step.4: set 8 min over low heat
>> step.5: flip onto a plate, slide back, 4 min
>> step.6: The turn is the whole trick and a plate does it. Cover the pan, take both in one grip, tip it over in a single movement rather than a nervous one, then slide the tortilla back in raw side down. Rest it five minutes before cutting; the middle should be barely set, not dry.

One pan, one bowl and a plate to turn it on. The oil is poured off rather than strained, and the potato is lifted straight into the beaten egg with a slotted spoon — nothing here needs a colander, which is the only reason a fried-potato dish can sit on this shelf at all.

Fry @Yukon Gold potatoes{1 1/2%lb}(700 g; peeled, sliced thin), @yellow onion{1%large}(sliced thin) in @olive oil{1 1/2%cups}(360 mL) in a #nonstick skillet{} for ~fry{20%min}, moving them so they soften without browning.

Beat @eggs{6}, @kosher salt{1 1/2%tsp} in a bowl, lift @&(~1)fried potatoes{} into them with a slotted spoon, pour off all but a spoonful of the oil, and ~stand{10%min}.

Set @&(~1)potato and egg{} back in the pan over low heat for ~fry{8%min}, pulling the edge in as it firms.

Flip @&(~1)tortilla{} onto a plate, slide it back in raw side down, and ~fry{4%min}.

The turn is the whole trick and a plate does it. Cover the pan, take both in one grip, tip it over in a single movement rather than a nervous one, then slide the tortilla back in raw side down. Rest it five minutes before cutting; the middle should be barely set, not dry.
